## Code quality

| Command | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| `dude lint` | Run all structural lint checks (naming, layout conventions) |
| `dude explain <CODE>` | Print the prose behind a lint code — why the rule exists and how to fix it |
| `dude format` | Format code — `ruff` (backend) + `prettier` (frontend) |
| `dude upgrade` | Update the pinned `dude` CLI and stack versions for this project |
| `dude review` | Run lint + ESLint + API contract review in one pass |

### Project lint rules

`dude lint` also runs any rules you define under `.dude/lint/checks/` — the
rule code is derived from the path (`.dude/lint/checks/PRJ/001.ts` → `PRJ001`)
and the contract is the same one the stack's own checks use. See
`.dude/lint/checks/README.md` and the `PRJ/001.ts` example. Document a rule with
a Markdown file next to it (`PRJ/001.md`) and `dude explain PRJ001` serves it.
To disable a built-in rule, list its code in `dude.json`:

```json
{ "lint": { "disable": ["BE003"] } }
```

### Verifying your work mechanically

`dude lint` and `dude explain` are designed to be used together — the fastest
loop for anyone, human or coding agent, working in this project:

```bash
dude lint --format json     # what broke, where, and under which rule code
dude explain BE003          # why that rule exists and how to satisfy it
```

`--format json` writes nothing but the JSON document to stdout, so it pipes
cleanly (`dude lint --format json | jq '.diagnostics[].code'`). Exit codes are
the same as the human format: non-zero only when there is at least one error.
Run `dude explain` with no code to list every rule that applies here.

### Upgrading pinned versions

`dude upgrade` updates version pins only. It does **not** migrate existing
project files.

| Command | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| `dude upgrade` | Upgrade both the CLI pin in `package.json` and the stack pin in `dude.json` |
| `dude upgrade --cli` | Upgrade only `@cubocicloide/dude` |
| `dude upgrade --stack` | Upgrade only the active stack version |
| `dude upgrade --cli --cli-version 0.6.1` | Pin the CLI to an explicit version |
| `dude upgrade --stack --stack-version 5.0.5` | Pin the stack to an explicit version |

If a newer version causes issues, roll back by pinning the previous one again:

```bash
dude upgrade --cli --cli-version 0.6.0
dude upgrade --stack --stack-version 5.0.4
pnpm install
```

